/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/securityx.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("securityx", [["sx1",0,1,"A CISO wants a mandatory, leadership-approved document stating that all sensitive data must be encrypted, while the specific algorithms and key lengths are allowed to change over time without board approval. Where should the algorithm requirements live?",["In a technical standard that supports the policy","In the policy itself, signed by the board","In an optional guideline for engineers","In the incident response plan"],0,"Policies state high-level mandatory intent and change rarely; standards make them measurable (for example AES-256) and can be updated by the security team. Putting algorithms in the policy would force board approval for every crypto change.","Domain 1: Governance components",[null,"Embedding algorithm details in the policy means every crypto update needs board re-approval, which is exactly what the CISO wants to avoid.","Guidelines are recommendations, so making encryption algorithms optional would not enforce the mandatory requirement.","The incident response plan covers handling incidents, not the ongoing encryption requirements for sensitive data."],null],["sx2",0,1,"A security program must show which team is accountable for approving firewall rule changes, who performs them, and who must be told afterward. Which tool best documents this?",["A risk register","A business impact analysis","A RACI matrix","A data flow diagram"],2,"A RACI matrix maps each activity to who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed, removing ambiguity about approval and execution.","Domain 1: Governance components",["A risk register tracks risks, their scores, owners and treatments, not who performs and approves each operational task.","A BIA identifies critical processes and their recovery requirements; it does not assign task-level roles.",null,"A data flow diagram shows how data moves between components, which is useful for threat modeling but not role assignment."],null],["sx3",0,1,"A risk assessment finds that a customer portal has a residual risk score of 16 after controls, while the board-approved appetite allows residual scores up to 12. The business owner wants to launch anyway. What is the correct governance step?",["Launch, because the inherent risk has already been lowered","Adjust the scoring method so the residual score drops to 12 and fits within the approved appetite","Transfer the decision to the security analyst who ran the assessment","Escalate for formal risk acceptance by an executive with authority, or apply more treatment first"],3,"Residual risk above appetite must be treated further or explicitly accepted by someone with the authority to exceed appetite, and the decision must be documented. Reducing inherent risk alone does not make it acceptable.","Domain 1: Risk management",["Lowering inherent risk is expected, but the residual score still exceeds appetite, so launching without a decision bypasses governance.","Changing the score without changing the controls falsifies the assessment and leaves the actual exposure the same.","Analysts assess and advise; accepting risk above appetite requires an owner or executive with authority.",null],null],["sx4",0,1,"An online retailer calculates that a web server outage would cost $40,000 per event and expects it about twice a year. A redundant design costs $50,000 per year to operate. Based on ALE alone, what should management conclude?",["The control costs more than the annual expected loss of the outage, so management should reject it","ALE cannot be calculated without the asset's purchase price","The SLE is $20,000, so no control is needed","The control costs less than the $80,000 annual loss expectancy, so it is justified"],3,"SLE is $40,000 and ARO is 2, so ALE = $80,000. A $50,000 annual control that removes most of that loss is cost-justified.","Domain 1: Risk management",["$50,000 per year is less than the $80,000 ALE, so the control is cheaper than the expected loss, not more.","ALE is SLE multiplied by ARO; both are given here, so the purchase price is not needed.","The SLE is the $40,000 loss per event; $20,000 is not derived from the figures given.",null],null],["sx5",0,1,"A company buys cyber insurance to cover the costs of ransomware recovery and legal claims. Which risk treatment is this?",["Transference","Avoidance","Mitigation","Acceptance"],0,"Insurance shifts part of the financial impact to a third party, which is risk transference. It does not reduce the likelihood of the event.","Domain 1: Risk management",[null,"Avoidance means stopping the risky activity altogether, such as not storing the data; buying insurance keeps the activity.","Mitigation adds controls that reduce likelihood or impact of the event itself, like backups or EDR.","Acceptance means knowingly taking on the risk without further action; paying for insurance is an action that shifts the cost."],null],["sx6",0,1,"A SaaS provider will process your customer records. Before signing, you need independent evidence that its controls operated effectively over a period of time. What should you request?",["A SOC 2 Type I report","The vendor's marketing security whitepaper","A SOC 2 Type II report","A self-completed questionnaire only"],2,"A SOC 2 Type II report is an independent auditor's opinion on the design and operating effectiveness of controls over a period (often 6 to 12 months). Type I covers design at a single point in time.","Domain 1: Third-party risk",["A Type I report only assesses control design at a single point in time, not whether controls worked over a period.","A whitepaper is written by the vendor for sales purposes and is not independent evidence.",null,"A questionnaire answered by the vendor is useful for scoping but is not independently verified."],null],["sx7",0,1,"After a widely used open-source logging library is found to be vulnerable, a company spends days finding which of its products include it. Which practice would have answered the question in minutes?",["Running annual penetration tests","Signing all code with a code-signing certificate","Increasing the SIEM log retention period","Requiring and maintaining an SBOM for each product"],3,"A software bill of materials lists every component and version in a product, so a query can show which products include an affected library right away.","Domain 1: Supply chain risk",["Annual penetration tests find exploitable issues at a point in time but do not inventory every embedded component.","Code signing proves who published code and that it was not altered; it does not list the dependencies inside.","Longer log retention helps investigations but does not tell you which products contain a library.",null],null],["sx8",0,1,"A business impact analysis shows that the order system can lose at most 15 minutes of transactions and must be back online within 2 hours. Which pair of metrics has been defined?",["RTO of 15 minutes and RPO of 2 hours","RPO of 15 minutes and RTO of 2 hours","MTBF of 15 minutes and MTTR of 2 hours","WRT of 15 minutes and MTD of 2 hours"],1,"Recovery point objective is the maximum tolerable data loss measured in time; recovery time objective is how quickly service must be restored.","Domain 1: Business continuity",["This swaps the definitions: data loss tolerance is the RPO and time to restore is the RTO.",null,"MTBF and MTTR are reliability measurements of components, not business-set recovery targets.","Work recovery time and maximum tolerable downtime are related BIA terms, but the data-loss limit is specifically the RPO."],null],["sx9",0,1,"A disaster recovery team wants to validate its plan by walking through a ransomware scenario around a conference table, without touching production systems. Which test type is this?",["Full interruption test","Tabletop exercise","Parallel test","Simulation with live failover"],1,"A tabletop exercise is a discussion-based walkthrough of roles and decisions. It is low risk and good for finding gaps in communication and procedures.","Domain 1: Business continuity",["A full interruption test actually shuts down primary operations and switches to recovery, which is the most disruptive test.",null,"A parallel test brings up the recovery site and runs it alongside production, which touches real systems.","Live failover exercises move real workloads, which goes beyond a discussion around a table."],null],["sx10",0,1,"A European company's customers ask it to delete all their personal data. Which regulation most directly gives them this right?",["GDPR","PCI DSS","SOX","HIPAA"],0,"The EU General Data Protection Regulation grants data subjects rights including access, rectification and erasure (the right to be forgotten), subject to exceptions.","Domain 1: Compliance",[null,"PCI DSS is an industry standard for protecting payment card data and does not grant individuals a right to erasure.","The Sarbanes-Oxley Act governs financial reporting controls of US public companies, not personal data deletion.","HIPAA governs protected health information in the US and gives patients access and amendment rights, not a general erasure right."],null],["sx11",0,1,"A multinational must keep citizens' records physically stored in their home country, and a cloud architect proposes a single global storage bucket in another region for cost savings. Which concern should block the design?",["Data sovereignty and localization requirements","Right to audit clauses","Separation of duties","Non-repudiation"],0,"Data sovereignty means data is subject to the laws of the country where it is stored, and some laws require it to stay in-country. A single foreign region would violate that.","Domain 1: Compliance",[null,"Right to audit clauses let a customer inspect a vendor's controls; they do not dictate where data is stored.","Separation of duties splits critical tasks between people and does not relate to storage location.","Non-repudiation proves who performed an action, typically with digital signatures, and is unrelated to data location."],null],["sx12",0,1,"A company wants a certifiable information security management system that an accredited auditor can certify. Which standard should it adopt?",["NIST CSF","ISO/IEC 27001","CIS Controls","ISO/IEC 27002"],1,"ISO/IEC 27001 defines the requirements for an ISMS, and organizations can be certified against it by accredited bodies. ISO/IEC 27002 gives control guidance but is not a certification standard.","Domain 1: Frameworks",["The NIST Cybersecurity Framework is a voluntary outcome framework; organizations do not receive an accredited certification against it.",null,"CIS Controls are a prioritized set of safeguards; they are not an ISMS standard with accredited certification.","ISO/IEC 27002 is guidance on implementing controls and cannot itself be certified against."],null],["sx13",0,1,"A US federal contractor must select and document security controls for a system from a large catalog organized by families such as AC, AU and IR. Which publication provides this catalog?",["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-61","ISO/IEC 27701","CSA STAR"],0,"NIST SP 800-53 is the control catalog with families such as Access Control (AC), Audit and Accountability (AU) and Incident Response (IR).","Domain 1: Frameworks",[null,"NIST SP 800-61 is the computer security incident handling guide, not a control catalog.","ISO/IEC 27701 extends ISO 27001 for privacy information management; it does not use AC/AU/IR families.","CSA STAR is a cloud provider assurance registry based on the Cloud Controls Matrix, not the federal control catalog."],null],["sx14",0,1,"Legal counsel informs IT that litigation is expected over a former employee's actions. Which action should IT take first regarding that employee's mailbox and files?",["Delete them under the normal retention schedule","Export them to a PST file and email copies to the whole legal team for review","Place them under a legal hold so retention policies cannot delete them","Reset the mailbox password and give it to HR"],2,"When litigation is reasonably anticipated, relevant data must be preserved. A legal hold suspends normal deletion so evidence is not destroyed (spoliation).","Domain 1: Legal and privacy",["Deleting data once litigation is anticipated can be treated as spoliation of evidence, with serious legal consequences.","Emailing copies spreads sensitive data, breaks chain of custody and does not stop the originals from being deleted.",null,"Sharing a reset password with HR undermines accountability and does not preserve the data from retention deletion."],null],["sx15",0,1,"During design review of a new payment API, the team goes through each element of the data flow diagram asking about spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service and elevation of privilege. Which method are they using?",["STRIDE","PASTA","DREAD","MITRE ATT&CK"],0,"STRIDE is a threat categorization mnemonic applied to each element of a data flow diagram at design time.","Domain 1: Threat modeling",[null,"PASTA is a seven-stage, risk-centric process that aligns threats to business impact rather than a per-element category checklist.","DREAD is a scoring model for rating threats (damage, reproducibility and so on), not a set of threat categories.","MITRE ATT&CK catalogs observed adversary tactics and techniques; it is not the six-category design checklist described."],null],["sx16",0,1,"A threat modeling team wants to reason about how real adversary groups have achieved persistence and lateral movement in similar companies, based on observed behavior. Which resource fits best?",["STRIDE","MITRE ATT&CK","CVSS","OWASP ASVS"],1,"ATT&CK is a knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques observed in real intrusions, including groups known to use them.","Domain 1: Threat modeling",["STRIDE categorizes design threats but does not describe how real adversary groups actually behaved.",null,"CVSS scores the severity of individual vulnerabilities, not adversary behavior across an intrusion.","OWASP ASVS is a list of application security verification requirements, not a catalog of observed attacker techniques."],null],["sx17",0,1,"Employees have started pasting customer contracts into a public generative AI chatbot to summarize them. What should the security team put in place first?",["A block on all internet access for the legal department and every other team handling contracts","An annual penetration test of the chatbot vendor","An AI acceptable use policy naming approved tools and allowed data, enforced with DLP","Longer passwords for employee accounts"],2,"Governance comes first: define which AI tools are approved and what data may be used, then enforce it with technical controls such as DLP or CASB policies. Blocking all internet access is disproportionate.","Domain 1: AI adoption",["Cutting off all internet access disrupts the business and does not address other departments or approved uses of AI.","You generally cannot pen test a public vendor without permission, and it would not stop staff from sharing data.",null,"Password length does nothing to prevent employees from pasting sensitive data into an external service."],null],["sx18",0,1,"An internal chatbot that summarizes support tickets begins revealing its hidden system instructions after a customer submits a ticket containing text that tells the model to ignore previous directions. Which risk has occurred?",["Model poisoning during training","SQL injection","Indirect prompt injection","Model inversion"],2,"Instructions hidden in untrusted content that the model processes (the ticket) override the intended behavior. That is indirect prompt injection; mitigations include isolating untrusted input, limiting tool permissions and filtering outputs.","Domain 1: AI adoption",["Model poisoning corrupts the training data or model weights; here the model was manipulated at run time by input text.","SQL injection targets a database query interpreter, not a language model's instructions.",null,"Model inversion tries to reconstruct training data from model outputs; it does not involve overriding instructions."],null],["sx19",0,2,"An e-commerce site runs in one cloud region. Leadership wants the site to keep serving customers if the entire region fails. Which design meets the requirement?",["A multi-region deployment with replicated data and DNS-based failover","Two instances in the same availability zone behind a load balancer with health checks","Nightly backups of the database stored in the same region","Larger instance sizes with more CPU and memory in the current region"],0,"Only geographic dispersion across regions survives a regional outage. Same-zone redundancy, same-region backups and vertical scaling all share the region as a single point of failure.","Domain 2: Resilient design",[null,"Both instances share one availability zone and region, so a regional failure takes them down together.","Backups in the same region are lost or unreachable with the region, and restoring from them is slow.","Vertical scaling adds capacity but leaves the whole site dependent on one region."],null],["sx20",0,2,"During a sudden spike in traffic, a streaming service disables personalized recommendations but keeps video playback working. Which resilience principle is this?",["Graceful degradation","Fail open","Horizontal scaling","Blue-green deployment"],0,"Graceful degradation keeps core functions available by shedding non-essential features under stress.","Domain 2: Resilient design",[null,"Fail open describes a control that allows traffic when it fails, such as a firewall passing everything; it is not about shedding features.","Horizontal scaling adds more instances to handle load; here the service reduced features instead of adding capacity.","Blue-green deployment is a release technique that switches between two environments, unrelated to load shedding."],null],["sx21",0,2,"An attacker who compromises one web server should not be able to reach other web servers in the same subnet. Which control addresses this east-west risk most directly?",["Microsegmentation with workload-level policies","A perimeter firewall at the internet edge","A larger subnet mask for the web tier","A web application firewall in front of the site"],0,"Microsegmentation enforces policy between individual workloads, even inside one subnet, limiting lateral movement.","Domain 2: Network architecture",[null,"The perimeter firewall inspects north-south traffic and does not see traffic between servers in the same subnet.","Changing the subnet mask only changes addressing; hosts in the same subnet still reach each other.","A WAF filters inbound HTTP requests to the application, not server-to-server traffic inside the network."],null],["sx22",0,2,"A hospital needs to ensure that only corporate devices with current patches and running EDR can join the wired network, while noncompliant devices land in a remediation VLAN. Which solution fits?",["MAC address filtering on each switch","A DMZ for all hospital workstations","Network access control with 802.1X and posture assessment","Static IP addressing for managed devices"],2,"NAC authenticates devices with 802.1X, checks posture (patches, EDR) and assigns VLANs dynamically, including a remediation VLAN.","Domain 2: Network architecture",["MAC filtering is easily spoofed and cannot check patch level or EDR status.","A DMZ (screened subnet) is for public-facing services, not for placing internal workstations.",null,"Static addresses do not verify device identity or health and are trivial to copy."],null],["sx23",0,2,"In a zero trust architecture, which component evaluates identity, device health and context to decide whether a request should be allowed?",["The policy enforcement point","The policy decision point (policy engine)","The certificate revocation list","The jump server"],1,"In the NIST SP 800-207 model the policy engine and policy administrator make up the policy decision point that decides; the policy enforcement point carries out the decision in front of the resource.","Domain 2: Zero trust",["The enforcement point sits in the data path and applies the decision, but it does not make it.",null,"A CRL lists revoked certificates; it can be one input to trust decisions but does not evaluate requests.","A jump server is an administrative access host, not the component that evaluates zero trust policy."],null],["sx24",0,2,"A company moving to zero trust wants remote users to reach only the specific internal apps they are authorized for, without placing them on the internal network. What should replace the full-tunnel VPN?",["A split-tunnel VPN into the same flat network","Port forwarding each application on the edge firewall","A shared administrator account for remote access","Zero trust network access (ZTNA) brokered per application"],3,"ZTNA grants access to individual applications after identity and device checks, without network-level access, reducing lateral movement.","Domain 2: Zero trust",["Split tunneling only changes which traffic goes through the VPN; users still land on the internal network.","Exposing each app directly to the internet enlarges the attack surface and gives no per-user verification.","Shared accounts remove individual accountability and violate least privilege.",null],null],["sx25",0,2,"A development team wants to catch hard-coded credentials and injection flaws in code before it is merged, without running the application. Which testing type fits?",["Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","Static application security testing (SAST)","Fuzzing the production API","A red team engagement"],1,"SAST analyzes source code or binaries without executing them, so it can run on every pull request.","Domain 2: Secure SDLC",["DAST requires a running application and tests it from the outside, usually later in the pipeline.",null,"Fuzzing production is risky and requires a running target; it also would not examine code before merge.","A red team tests the organization's detection and response, not each code change before merge."],null],["sx26",0,2,"A build pipeline pulls dozens of open-source packages. Security wants to be alerted to known vulnerable versions and license conflicts. Which tool category addresses this?",["Interactive application security testing (IAST)","Software composition analysis (SCA)","Runtime application self-protection (RASP)","Host-based intrusion detection (HIDS)"],1,"SCA inventories third-party components, matches them against vulnerability databases and flags license issues, and can produce an SBOM.","Domain 2: Secure SDLC",["IAST instruments a running app during tests to find flaws in its behavior; it is not focused on dependency inventories and licenses.",null,"RASP protects an application at run time by blocking attacks; it does not report vulnerable dependency versions in builds.","A HIDS monitors a host for suspicious activity and file changes, not package versions in a build."],null],["sx27",0,2,"Attackers modified a build server script so that signed releases contained a backdoor. Which control most directly reduces this CI/CD risk?",["Running a full DAST scan against every signed release after it has been deployed to production servers","Adding a longer password to the source repository","Moving the build server into the DMZ","Isolated, hardened build runners with signed build provenance and reviewed pipeline changes"],3,"Protecting the integrity of the build itself (isolated ephemeral runners, reviewed pipeline changes, provenance attestations) prevents tampering that signing alone cannot catch, because the backdoor was signed legitimately.","Domain 2: Secure SDLC",["DAST tests application behavior from outside and is unlikely to detect a stealthy backdoor inserted at build time.","A stronger repository password does not protect the build server that was actually tampered with.","Placing the build server in a DMZ exposes it more, rather than isolating and protecting it.",null],null],["sx28",0,2,"A WAF in blocking mode is rejecting legitimate customer checkout requests that contain apostrophes in street addresses. What is the best way to resolve it?",["Add a narrow exception for that field to the specific rule and keep blocking mode","Switch the WAF to detection-only mode permanently","Remove the WAF and rely on the network firewall","Disable the entire SQL injection rule group on the checkout application and its APIs"],0,"Troubleshooting controls means fixing the false positive precisely, such as excluding one parameter from one rule, while keeping protection for everything else.","Domain 2: Integrating controls",[null,"Permanent detection-only mode stops blocking real attacks everywhere to fix one field.","A network firewall cannot inspect HTTP payloads, so removing the WAF drops application-layer protection.","Disabling the whole rule group removes SQL injection protection from every page, not just the address field."],null],["sx29",0,2,"The SOC cannot correlate firewall and server logs because events appear out of order by several minutes. What should be fixed first?",["Synchronize every log source to a common NTP time source","Increase the SIEM license so it can accept more events per second","Convert all logs to CSV before ingestion","Shorten the log retention period"],0,"Correlation depends on accurate timestamps. Time drift between sources makes events appear out of order; NTP synchronization (and consistent time zones) fixes it.","Domain 2: Integrating controls",[null,"More licensed capacity does not change the timestamps that are wrong at the source.","Changing the file format does not correct clock drift on the devices.","Retention controls how long logs are kept and has nothing to do with ordering."],null],["sx30",0,2,"A proxy deployed for TLS inspection breaks a banking application that uses certificate pinning. What is the most appropriate fix?",["Disable certificate pinning on every client device","Add a bypass for that application's destinations in the inspection policy","Install the bank's private key on the proxy","Turn off TLS inspection for all outbound traffic across the organization"],1,"Pinned apps reject the proxy's substitute certificate by design. A targeted inspection bypass for trusted destinations is the standard fix, keeping inspection elsewhere.","Domain 2: Integrating controls",["Pinning is set by the app developer and cannot generally be disabled by the customer; weakening it would also reduce security.",null,"You cannot obtain a third party's private key, and doing so would be a serious compromise of their security.","Turning off all inspection gives up visibility across the whole organization to fix one app."],null],["sx31",0,2,"A company wants to stop employees from emailing documents labeled Confidential to personal addresses, and to log attempts. Which control fits best?",["Data loss prevention policies that use classification labels","Full-disk encryption on laptops","Network address translation at the edge","An IDS signature for SMTP traffic"],0,"DLP inspects content and labels in motion and can block, quarantine or log sending of classified data to unapproved destinations.","Domain 2: Data security",[null,"Full-disk encryption protects data at rest on a lost device, not data an authorized user sends by email.","NAT translates addresses and has no awareness of document labels.","An IDS signature looks for attack patterns, not for sensitive business content leaving by email."],null],["sx32",0,2,"A payment system must let a fraud-analytics platform correlate transactions by card number without that platform ever storing real card numbers. Which technique fits?",["Static data masking of the card numbers","Hashing card numbers with MD5","Base64 encoding of the card numbers","Tokenization with a secured token vault"],3,"Tokens are consistent substitutes, so analytics can correlate by token, and only the vault can map back to the real number, reducing PCI DSS scope for the analytics platform.","Domain 2: Data security",["Static masking typically replaces or hides digits irreversibly, which can break correlation and cannot be reversed for authorized use.","Unsalted MD5 of a card number is weak because the card number space is small enough to brute-force.","Base64 is encoding, not protection; anyone can decode it to the real card number.",null],null],["sx33",0,2,"A company wants employees to sign in once with corporate credentials to several SaaS apps, with the identity provider sending signed XML assertions. Which protocol is being used?",["OAuth 2.0","Kerberos","RADIUS","SAML 2.0"],3,"SAML 2.0 exchanges signed XML assertions between an identity provider and service providers for web SSO.","Domain 2: Identity architecture",["OAuth 2.0 issues access tokens for delegated authorization and does not use XML assertions for SSO.","Kerberos uses tickets from a KDC inside a domain, not XML assertions to SaaS providers.","RADIUS provides AAA for network access such as VPN and Wi-Fi, not browser-based SaaS SSO.",null],null],["sx34",0,2,"A mobile app needs to call a user's calendar API on their behalf without ever handling the user's password. Which approach is correct?",["Storing the user's password in the app and sending it with each call","OAuth 2.0 implicit flow with long-lived tokens","OAuth 2.0 authorization code flow with PKCE","Sharing a single API key embedded in the app"],2,"OAuth 2.0 delegates scoped access with tokens. For public clients like mobile apps, the authorization code flow with PKCE is the recommended pattern; the implicit flow is deprecated in current guidance.","Domain 2: Identity architecture",["Storing and replaying passwords is exactly what delegated authorization exists to avoid, and it exposes the credential.","The implicit flow returns tokens in the browser redirect and is no longer recommended; long-lived tokens make it worse.",null,"A shared embedded key can be extracted from the app and does not identify or limit access per user."],null],["sx35",0,2,"Administrators currently hold permanent Domain Admin rights. Leadership wants them to request elevation for a limited time with approval and session recording. Which solution fits?",["A longer password policy for administrators","A single shared admin account with a vaulted password","Privileged access management with just-in-time elevation","Role-based access with permanent group membership"],2,"PAM with just-in-time access grants privileges only when needed, for a limited time, with approval workflows and session recording, removing standing privilege.","Domain 2: Identity architecture",["Longer passwords do not remove standing privileges or add approval and recording.","A shared account removes individual accountability even if its password is vaulted.",null,"Permanent group membership is the standing privilege leadership wants to remove."],null],["sx36",0,2,"Conditional access should block sign-ins from unmanaged devices to finance apps but allow them to the intranet with MFA. Which inputs are the policy using?",["Only the user's password strength","Device compliance state, application sensitivity and user identity","Only the source IP address","The user's job title and office phone number"],1,"Conditional access evaluates signals such as identity, device health or management, application and risk to decide whether to allow, require MFA or block.","Domain 2: Identity architecture",["Password strength is not evaluated at sign-in and does not tell the policy whether the device is managed.",null,"Source IP alone cannot distinguish managed from unmanaged devices.","Job title and phone number are directory attributes, not the device and app signals the policy described relies on."],null],["sx37",0,2,"In an IaaS deployment, who is responsible for patching the guest operating system on a virtual machine?",["The customer","The cloud provider","The hypervisor vendor","Shared equally, depending on the region"],0,"Under the shared responsibility model, IaaS customers manage the guest OS, applications, data and identity configuration; the provider secures the physical hosts and hypervisor.","Domain 2: Cloud architecture",[null,"The provider patches physical infrastructure and the hypervisor, not the customer's guest OS in IaaS.","The hypervisor vendor maintains the virtualization layer and has no role in patching customer guest operating systems.","Responsibility splits by service model, not by region, and guest OS patching is the customer's in IaaS."],null],["sx38",0,2,"A cloud team discovers storage buckets set to public and security groups open to 0.0.0.0/0 on SSH across many accounts. Which tool continuously finds and reports these misconfigurations?",["Cloud security posture management (CSPM)","A cloud access security broker in forward-proxy mode","A web application firewall","A hardware security module"],0,"CSPM continuously checks cloud configurations against benchmarks and policies and flags risky settings such as public buckets and open management ports.","Domain 2: Cloud architecture",[null,"A forward-proxy CASB governs user access to SaaS, not the configuration of IaaS resources in your accounts.","A WAF filters HTTP requests to applications and cannot see bucket permissions or security group rules.","An HSM stores and uses cryptographic keys and does not scan cloud configurations."],null],["sx39",0,2,"A company wants cloud-delivered secure web gateway, CASB, ZTNA and firewall services combined with SD-WAN for its branch offices. Which architecture is this?",["A screened subnet","A hub-and-spoke MPLS WAN","Secure access service edge (SASE)","A virtual desktop infrastructure"],2,"SASE converges networking (SD-WAN) with cloud-delivered security services (SSE: SWG, CASB, ZTNA, FWaaS).","Domain 2: Cloud architecture",["A screened subnet is a network zone for public-facing services, not a cloud-delivered security service bundle.","MPLS hub and spoke is a WAN topology that backhauls traffic; it does not include the listed security services.",null,"VDI hosts desktops centrally and does not provide SD-WAN or web security services."],null],["sx40",0,2,"A container image in production is found to contain an AWS access key in an environment file. What is the best long-term fix?",["Base64-encode the key in the Dockerfile","Move the key into a different layer of the image","Make the container registry private and restrict who can pull the image","Inject secrets at run time from a secrets manager and scan images in CI"],3,"Secrets should never be baked into images. Pull them at run time from a secrets manager, rotate the exposed key and add secret scanning to the build.","Domain 2: Containers",["Base64 is reversible encoding and hides nothing from anyone who pulls the image.","Every layer is part of the image and can be extracted, so moving it to another layer does not remove it.","A private registry limits who can pull images, but anyone with pull access still gets the key.",null],null],["sx41",0,2,"A Kubernetes cluster runs workloads from several teams. Which measure best limits the damage if one pod is compromised?",["Giving every pod cluster-admin rights for easier troubleshooting","Running all workloads in the default namespace","Exposing the API server publicly for convenience","Network policies, least-privilege service accounts and blocking privileged containers"],3,"Restricting pod-to-pod traffic, limiting RBAC for service accounts and enforcing pod security standards (no privileged containers) contain a compromised pod.","Domain 2: Containers",["cluster-admin on every pod gives an attacker full control of the cluster from any compromised workload.","Putting all workloads in one namespace removes an isolation and policy boundary between teams.","A publicly exposed API server increases the attack surface of the control plane.",null],null],["sx42",0,2,"A company runs workloads in two public clouds and on premises. Auditors find that each environment has different encryption key policies and rotation schedules. What design change best addresses this?",["Letting each cloud team choose keys independently","Central key management with one consistent policy integrated with each cloud","Disabling encryption in the smaller cloud","Using the same static encryption key in all three environments so the policy is identical"],1,"Centralized key management and policy across environments give consistent rotation, access control and auditing, which is a core multicloud design concern.","Domain 2: Hybrid and multicloud",["Independent choices are what created the inconsistent policies the auditors flagged.",null,"Disabling encryption removes protection and likely violates compliance requirements.","One static shared key has no rotation and turns a single compromise into a breach of every environment."],null],["sx43",0,3,"After a planned certificate renewal on the identity provider, every SAML sign-in to a SaaS app fails with an invalid signature error, while other federated apps work. What is the most likely cause?",["The SaaS app still trusts the old IdP signing certificate","The users' passwords expired at the same time","The SaaS app's DNS records were deleted","The users' MFA authenticator devices lost time synchronization with the IdP"],0,"The service provider validates assertion signatures against the IdP certificate it has stored. When the IdP rotates its signing certificate, each SP must be updated with the new certificate or metadata.","Domain 3: Troubleshooting IAM",[null,"Expired passwords would fail at the IdP login step, not produce a signature validation error at one SaaS app.","Missing DNS records would stop the app loading at all rather than returning a signature error.","MFA time drift would fail authentication at the IdP for all apps, not signature validation at one app."],null],["sx44",0,3,"Users report that their TOTP codes from an authenticator app are rejected intermittently on one RADIUS server only. The other RADIUS server works. What should be checked first?",["The users' password complexity","The TLS version on the corporate website","Clock synchronization (NTP) on the failing RADIUS server","The DHCP lease time on the user VLAN"],2,"TOTP codes depend on time. If one validating server's clock drifts, codes fall outside its acceptance window while the other server still accepts them.","Domain 3: Troubleshooting IAM",["Password complexity does not affect whether a time-based one-time code is accepted.","The website's TLS version has nothing to do with RADIUS validation of TOTP codes.",null,"DHCP lease times affect addressing on clients, not the validation window on a server."],null],["sx45",0,3,"Certificate-based smart card logons fail for all users after an internal CA's CRL distribution point server was decommissioned. What is happening?",["The users' private keys were erased from their smart cards during the server change","The smart card readers need new firmware","Domain controllers cannot retrieve the CRL, so certificate validation fails","Kerberos was disabled on the domain"],2,"When revocation checking is required and the CRL cannot be retrieved, validation fails closed. Publishing the CRL at a reachable location (or an OCSP responder) restores logons.","Domain 3: Troubleshooting IAM",["Decommissioning a CRL server does not touch keys stored on users' smart cards.","Reader firmware is unrelated to a server being removed and would not break all users at the same moment.",null,"Nothing in the change disabled Kerberos, and smart card logon itself uses Kerberos PKINIT."],null],["sx46",0,3,"A security team wants to ensure that only approved executables can run on point-of-sale terminals, which have a fixed software set. Which control is most effective?",["Signature-based antivirus alone","Application allow listing","A host-based firewall","Disk quotas"],1,"On fixed-function systems an allow list blocks anything not explicitly approved, including new malware that signatures do not know.","Domain 3: Endpoint hardening",["Signature-based antivirus only catches known malware and misses new or modified samples.",null,"A host firewall controls network connections, not which programs can execute.","Disk quotas limit storage use and do not prevent code from running."],null],["sx47",0,3,"An engineer must harden 200 new Linux servers consistently and prove they match the approved configuration. Which approach is best?",["Apply a CIS Benchmark baseline with configuration management and scan for drift","Harden each server by hand using a printed checklist signed off by a second engineer","Rely on the vendor's default installation settings","Disable logging to reduce the attack surface"],0,"Codifying a benchmark-based baseline in configuration management applies it consistently, and regular compliance scans detect drift.","Domain 3: Endpoint hardening",[null,"Manual hardening of 200 servers is slow, error-prone and hard to prove consistent.","Default settings favor compatibility over security and usually leave unnecessary services enabled.","Disabling logging removes detection and audit evidence; it does not meaningfully reduce the attack surface."],null],["sx48",0,3,"EDR alerts show that an office document spawned PowerShell, which then made an outbound connection. What capability allowed EDR to detect this when antivirus did not?",["File hash matching against known malware","Full-disk encryption","Scheduled vulnerability scanning","Behavioral monitoring of process relationships and actions"],3,"EDR records process trees, command lines and network activity, so it can flag suspicious behavior such as an Office app launching a script interpreter, even without a known signature.","Domain 3: Endpoint hardening",["Hash matching only identifies files already known as malicious; the behavior here involves legitimate binaries.","Full-disk encryption protects data at rest and provides no visibility into running processes.","Vulnerability scanners find missing patches and misconfigurations, not live malicious behavior.",null],null],["sx49",0,3,"A company wants laptops to prove to a network access server that their boot chain has not been modified before they get network access. Which technology provides the evidence?",["BIOS password","Full-disk encryption with a passphrase only","Measured boot with TPM-based remote attestation","UEFI legacy compatibility mode"],2,"Measured boot records hashes of each boot component in TPM PCRs; remote attestation sends a signed quote of those measurements to a verifier.","Domain 3: Hardware security",["A BIOS password restricts firmware settings but produces no evidence of boot integrity.","A passphrase-only disk encryption setup protects data but does not measure or attest to the boot chain.",null,"Legacy compatibility mode disables Secure Boot protections, weakening boot integrity rather than proving it."],null],["sx50",0,3,"A payment processor needs tamper-resistant hardware to generate and use thousands of keys for transaction signing, with FIPS 140 validation. Which should it deploy?",["Trusted Platform Module (TPM)","Self-encrypting drives","A software key store on the application server","Hardware security module (HSM)"],3,"HSMs are dedicated, tamper-resistant devices (or cloud services) built for high-volume key generation and cryptographic operations, commonly FIPS 140 validated.","Domain 3: Hardware security",["A TPM is a per-device chip designed for platform keys and attestation, not high-volume transaction signing for many applications.","Self-encrypting drives protect data on that disk and do not provide a signing service.","A software key store keeps keys in server memory and files, which lacks tamper resistance.",null],null],["sx51",0,3,"Secure Boot prevents a server from loading a modified bootloader. How does it do this?",["The TPM encrypts the bootloader with a random key","The OS antivirus scans the bootloader after boot","The firmware compares the bootloader's file size and timestamp with values stored in NVRAM","Firmware verifies the bootloader's signature against trusted keys before running it"],3,"Secure Boot checks each boot component's signature against keys in the firmware databases and refuses to run unsigned or untrusted code. Measured boot, by contrast, records measurements rather than blocking.","Domain 3: Hardware security",["The TPM stores measurements and keys; it does not encrypt the bootloader as part of Secure Boot.","Antivirus runs after the OS loads, which is too late to stop a malicious bootloader.","File size and timestamp checks are trivial to spoof; Secure Boot uses cryptographic signatures.",null],null],["sx52",0,3,"A water utility's PLCs run an unsupported OS that the vendor will not let the utility patch. Which is the best compensating control strategy?",["Install the latest desktop antivirus on each PLC","Connect the PLCs directly to the corporate network so central IT tools can manage them","Reboot the PLCs weekly to clear any malware","Place them in a restricted OT zone with monitored remote access and passive monitoring"],3,"When patching is not possible, isolate and monitor: zones and conduits (as in ISA/IEC 62443 and the Purdue model), controlled remote access and passive OT monitoring that does not disrupt operations.","Domain 3: Specialized systems",["PLCs usually cannot run desktop antivirus, and installing unapproved software can void vendor support or disrupt control.","Connecting PLCs to the corporate network greatly expands exposure to IT-borne threats.","Frequent reboots threaten availability and safety, and persistent threats can survive them.",null],null],["sx53",0,3,"A factory must send sensor data from its OT network to corporate analytics while guaranteeing that nothing can be sent back into the OT network. Which technology fits?",["A bidirectional site-to-site VPN","A jump box with RDP","A unidirectional gateway (data diode)","A shared VLAN trunk between OT and IT"],2,"A data diode physically allows traffic in one direction only, so data can leave the OT network while inbound paths do not exist.","Domain 3: Specialized systems",["A VPN carries traffic both ways, so it cannot guarantee that nothing enters the OT network.","A jump box provides interactive access into the network, which is the opposite of the requirement.",null,"Trunking VLANs between OT and IT creates a two-way path and weakens segmentation."],null],["sx54",0,3,"In industrial control systems, which security objective usually takes top priority?",["Availability and safety of the physical process","Confidentiality of engineering documents","Anonymity of operators","Non-repudiation of emails"],0,"OT environments prioritize availability and safety because downtime or unexpected behavior can harm people, equipment and the environment. This is why changes are cautious and scans are carefully controlled.","Domain 3: Specialized systems",[null,"Confidentiality matters, but in OT it usually ranks below keeping the physical process running safely.","Operator anonymity is not a goal; accountability for control actions is usually wanted.","Email non-repudiation is a general IT concern and not the top priority of control systems."],null],["sx55",0,3,"A security team keeps finding servers where engineers changed firewall rules by hand, making them differ from the Terraform definitions. What is this called, and how should it be handled?",["Configuration drift; detect it with scans and reapply the code-defined state","Privilege escalation; remove the engineers' accounts and reset every admin password","Shadow IT; buy a CASB","Race condition; add locking to the application"],0,"Drift is when real configuration diverges from the declared infrastructure as code. Regular plan runs or compliance scans detect it, and changes should go through code review and the pipeline.","Domain 3: Automation",[null,"Privilege escalation is gaining rights beyond those granted; these engineers used access they had.","Shadow IT is unsanctioned services outside IT's control; a CASB would not reconcile Terraform state.","A race condition is a timing flaw in code, not a mismatch between declared and actual configuration."],null],["sx56",0,3,"A SOC wants phishing reports to be triaged automatically: extract URLs, check reputation, quarantine matching emails across mailboxes and open a ticket. Which technology fits?",["A vulnerability scanner","A SOAR playbook","A network TAP","A DLP endpoint agent"],1,"Security orchestration, automation and response tools run playbooks that call multiple tools' APIs, reducing repetitive analyst work.","Domain 3: Automation",["A vulnerability scanner identifies weaknesses on hosts and cannot orchestrate email triage.",null,"A TAP copies network traffic for monitoring and performs no actions.","A DLP agent watches data leaving endpoints and does not triage reported emails."],null],["sx57",0,3,"An engineer writes a Python script to disable accounts listed in a CSV. Before running it in production, what is the most important safeguard?",["Run it with domain admin rights to avoid errors","Test it in a non-production environment with a dry-run mode and peer review","Schedule it to run during peak business hours","Remove all logging to improve performance"],1,"Automation can cause widespread outages quickly. Testing, dry runs, code review and least-privilege service accounts reduce that risk.","Domain 3: Automation",["Excessive privilege increases the damage if the script has a bug or the CSV is wrong.",null,"Running during peak hours maximizes the impact of any mistake.","Logging is needed to audit what the script changed and to recover from errors."],null],["sx58",0,3,"A government agency is concerned that encrypted traffic captured today could be decrypted years from now. Which planning step addresses this concern?",["Switch from AES-256 to 3DES","Inventory cryptography and plan migration to post-quantum algorithms such as ML-KEM","Increase all RSA key sizes to 4096 bits and treat that as the complete long-term fix","Use MD5 for integrity checks"],1,"This is the harvest-now, decrypt-later threat. Quantum computers would break RSA and elliptic curve key exchange, so agencies build crypto agility and migrate to NIST post-quantum standards such as ML-KEM (FIPS 203).","Domain 3: Advanced cryptography",["3DES is a deprecated, weaker cipher, and symmetric algorithms were not the main quantum concern anyway.",null,"Bigger RSA keys do not provide lasting protection against a large quantum computer running Shor's algorithm.","MD5 is broken for collision resistance and does nothing about future decryption of traffic."],null],["sx59",0,3,"A web application must store user passwords so that offline cracking of a stolen database is slow. Which approach is best?",["SHA-256 with no salt","AES-256 encryption of each password with the key stored in the same database","A salted, deliberately slow hash such as Argon2id, bcrypt or PBKDF2","A reversible Base64 encoding"],2,"Key stretching functions are deliberately slow and use unique salts, so precomputed tables fail and each guess costs significant compute.","Domain 3: Advanced cryptography",["Unsalted fast hashes are vulnerable to rainbow tables and extremely fast GPU cracking.","Encryption is reversible, and keeping the key in the same database gives an attacker everything at once.",null,"Base64 is an encoding that anyone can reverse instantly."],null],["sx60",0,3,"An auditor asks how the company ensures that a stolen server private key cannot decrypt previously captured TLS sessions. What is the correct answer?",["The servers use ephemeral (EC)DHE key exchange for forward secrecy","The servers use RSA key transport to protect each session key with the certificate key","The certificates have a 10-year validity period","The servers compress data before encrypting it"],0,"With ephemeral Diffie-Hellman, each session key is derived from temporary values that are discarded, so the long-term key alone cannot recover past sessions. TLS 1.3 uses only forward-secret key exchange.","Domain 3: Advanced cryptography",[null,"With RSA key transport, the session key is encrypted with the server's long-term key, so stealing it decrypts recorded sessions.","A long validity period increases exposure and has nothing to do with protecting past sessions.","Compression before encryption can even leak information (as in CRIME) and does not provide forward secrecy."],null],["sx61",0,3,"A research firm wants a cloud provider to compute statistics on encrypted data without ever decrypting it. Which technique fits?",["Envelope encryption","Tokenization","Homomorphic encryption","Steganography"],2,"Homomorphic encryption allows certain computations on ciphertext, with results that decrypt to the same answer as computing on plaintext. It is computationally expensive but protects data in use.","Domain 3: Advanced cryptography",["Envelope encryption wraps data keys with a master key; data must still be decrypted to compute on it.","Tokenization substitutes values with tokens, and statistics cannot be computed on the underlying values.",null,"Steganography hides the existence of data within other data; it does not enable computation on it."],null],["sx62",0,3,"A cloud application encrypts each file with its own data key, and that data key is encrypted by a master key held in the provider's KMS. What is this pattern called?",["Key escrow","Envelope encryption","Perfect forward secrecy","Key stretching"],1,"Envelope encryption uses data encryption keys for bulk data, wrapped by a key encryption key in a KMS or HSM. Rotating the master key only requires re-wrapping data keys.","Domain 3: Advanced cryptography",["Key escrow stores a copy of keys with a trusted party for recovery, which is a different concept.",null,"Forward secrecy concerns session keys in key exchange protocols, not wrapping file keys.","Key stretching slows password-derived keys; it does not describe wrapping data keys with a master key."],null],["sx63",0,3,"A software vendor wants customers to verify that an installer came from the vendor and was not altered after release. Which control provides this?",["Encrypting the installer with AES","Publishing the installer over HTTP","Code signing with a certificate from a trusted CA","Compressing the installer into a ZIP file"],2,"A digital signature over the code proves the publisher's identity and integrity; the OS verifies the signature and certificate chain before running it.","Domain 3: Cryptographic use cases",["Encryption provides confidentiality but, on its own, does not prove who published the file.","Plain HTTP allows modification in transit and provides no origin proof.",null,"Compression changes the size of the file and gives no integrity or authenticity assurance."],null],["sx64",0,3,"Two microservices must authenticate each other on every API call, even inside the private network. Which control fits?",["Server-only TLS with a public CA certificate","Mutual TLS with certificates issued to each service","IP allow lists only","HTTP basic authentication over plain HTTP"],1,"In mutual TLS both client and server present certificates, so each proves its identity, and traffic is encrypted. Service meshes often automate this.","Domain 3: PKI",["Server-only TLS authenticates the server to the client but not the calling service.",null,"IP allow lists can be bypassed from a compromised host inside the range and do not prove service identity.","Basic auth over plain HTTP sends credentials in clear text on every call."],null],["sx65",0,3,"A company's issuing CA was compromised. Which PKI design choice limits the damage most?",["A single online root CA that issues all certificates","Certificates with no expiration date","Disabling revocation checking on clients to reduce load on the issuing CA","An offline root CA with separate online intermediate issuing CAs"],3,"With an offline root, the organization can revoke the compromised intermediate and issue a new one without rebuilding trust from the root.","Domain 3: PKI",["If the only CA is an online root and it is compromised, the entire trust hierarchy must be rebuilt.","Non-expiring certificates stay valid forever, making compromises last longer.","Without revocation checking, certificates from the compromised CA cannot be effectively distrusted.",null],null],["sx66",0,3,"Clients checking certificate revocation are slow because they download a large CRL. The team also wants to avoid each client contacting the CA directly. Which solution fits?",["Increasing the CRL publication interval to one month","Removing the CRL distribution point from certificates","OCSP stapling on the web servers","Using self-signed certificates on each server"],2,"With OCSP stapling, the server fetches a signed, time-stamped OCSP response and presents it in the TLS handshake, so clients get fresh status without downloading CRLs or contacting the CA.","Domain 3: PKI",["Publishing less often makes revocation information staler without making it smaller or faster.","Removing the CDP stops clients from checking revocation at all.",null,"Self-signed certificates break the trust chain and have no revocation infrastructure."],null],["sx67",0,3,"A company's domain is being spoofed in phishing emails. It has SPF and DKIM in place. Which DNS record tells receiving servers to reject messages that fail alignment and send reports?",["A DMARC record with p=reject","An MX record with a lower preference","A PTR record for the mail server","A CNAME for the domain's website"],0,"DMARC builds on SPF and DKIM, checks alignment with the visible From domain and publishes a policy (none, quarantine or reject) plus reporting addresses. Organizations usually move from p=none to p=reject in stages.","Domain 3: Email and DNS security",[null,"MX records direct inbound mail delivery; their preference values do not tell receivers to reject spoofed mail.","A PTR record maps an IP to a name for reverse lookups; it does not publish a spoofing policy.","A CNAME aliases one hostname to another and has no role in email authentication."],null],["sx68",0,3,"An organization wants resolvers to be able to verify that DNS answers for its zone are authentic and unmodified. What should it deploy?",["DNS over HTTPS on client browsers","DNSSEC signing of the zone","A longer TTL on all records","Split-horizon DNS"],1,"DNSSEC adds digital signatures to DNS records, and validating resolvers verify them through a chain of trust, preventing forged answers such as cache poisoning.","Domain 3: Email and DNS security",["DoH encrypts the query path between client and resolver but does not prove the zone data is authentic.",null,"A longer TTL affects caching duration, not authenticity.","Split-horizon returns different answers to internal and external clients; it provides no signatures."],null],["sx69",0,3,"Employees use personal phones for corporate email. The company wants to wipe only corporate data if an employee leaves, without touching personal photos. Which capability fits?",["MDM/MAM containerization with selective wipe","Full device wipe of every phone","Disabling the phone camera","Requiring a simple four-digit PIN"],0,"Containerization separates corporate apps and data from personal content, so a selective (enterprise) wipe removes only the corporate container.","Domain 3: Mobile management",[null,"A full wipe would erase personal data, which is not acceptable for BYOD.","Disabling the camera limits a feature but does nothing to remove corporate data.","A simple PIN protects the lock screen but does not separate or remove corporate data."],null],["sx70",0,3,"A network team manages routers with SNMPv2c and community string public. Which change most improves the security of monitoring?",["Change the community string to private","Move SNMP to TCP port 443","Enable SNMP write access for faster changes","Migrate to SNMPv3 with authentication and privacy (authPriv)"],3,"SNMPv3 in authPriv mode adds user-based authentication and encryption; v2c community strings are sent in clear text.","Domain 3: Network infrastructure",["private is also a well-known default and still travels in clear text with v2c.","Changing the port does not add authentication or encryption.","Write access lets anyone with the string change device configuration, increasing risk.",null],null],["sx71",0,4,"A SIEM rule fires hundreds of times a day for failed logins from a vulnerability scanner's IP address during authorized scans. What is the best tuning action?",["Disable the failed-login rule entirely","Delete the scanner's logs before ingestion","Raise the alert threshold to 10,000 failed logins per day for every source","Add a narrow, documented exception for the scanner during its scan window"],3,"Precise, documented exceptions remove known-good noise while keeping the rule effective for everyone else.","Domain 4: Monitoring",["Disabling the rule would blind the SOC to real brute-force and password-spraying activity.","Dropping the scanner's logs removes evidence and could hide misuse of the scanner itself.","A huge global threshold would hide genuine attacks from every other source.",null],null],["sx72",0,4,"A SIEM correlation rule should flag a likely account compromise. Which pattern is the strongest signal?",["One failed login followed by a success from the same workstation","A user logging in at 9 a.m. on a weekday","Repeated failures, a success from a new country, then a new mail forwarding rule","A password change through the normal self-service portal after the 90-day expiry reminder"],2,"Correlating several weak signals in sequence (brute force, anomalous success, post-compromise action) yields a high-fidelity alert.","Domain 4: Monitoring",["A single typo followed by a success from the usual workstation is normal user behavior.","Logging in at the start of the workday is expected activity.",null,"Self-service password changes through the approved portal are routine."],null],["sx73",0,4,"Two organizations want to exchange threat intelligence automatically in a standard structured format over an API. Which pair of standards fits?",["SAML for the data format and LDAP for transport","STIX for the data format and TAXII for transport","CVSS for the data format and SNMP for transport","YARA for the data format and Syslog for transport"],1,"STIX (Structured Threat Information Expression) describes indicators, TTPs and relationships; TAXII defines how to exchange STIX over HTTPS.","Domain 4: Threat intelligence",["SAML carries authentication assertions and LDAP queries directories; neither is designed for threat intelligence.",null,"CVSS scores vulnerability severity and SNMP manages network devices; neither shares intelligence.","YARA describes file-matching rules and Syslog carries log messages; together they are not a sharing standard."],null],["sx74",0,4,"Why do detections based on adversary TTPs tend to remain useful longer than those based on file hashes or IP addresses?",["TTPs are always published by vendors within minutes of a new campaign starting","Hashes are too long for SIEM storage","IP addresses cannot be logged by firewalls","Behaviors cost attackers more to change than individual indicators do"],3,"Per the Pyramid of Pain, attackers can change hashes, IPs and domains cheaply, but changing tools and techniques costs them much more.","Domain 4: Threat intelligence",["TTP intelligence is not always shared quickly, and speed of sharing is not why it lasts.","Hashes are small and easily stored; the issue is that attackers can change them easily.","Firewalls routinely log IP addresses; the weakness is that attackers rotate them.",null],null],["sx75",0,4,"A threat hunter reads a report that an adversary group uses scheduled tasks for persistence and decides to search endpoint telemetry for unusual task creation. What type of hunt is this?",["Automated signature scanning","Hypothesis-driven, intelligence-based hunting","A vulnerability assessment","A compliance audit"],1,"The hunt starts from a testable hypothesis informed by threat intelligence, then searches data for evidence. Findings should become automated detections.","Domain 4: Threat hunting",["Signature scanning is automated matching of known patterns, not an analyst-led search from a hypothesis.",null,"Vulnerability assessments look for weaknesses, not evidence of attacker activity.","Compliance audits check controls against requirements, not for signs of compromise."],null],["sx76",0,4,"A UEBA system flags an engineer who normally downloads 50 MB per day from the code repository but downloaded 40 GB the day after giving notice. What is the best next step?",["Investigate the activity under the insider threat procedure with HR and legal","Ignore it, because the engineer has legitimate repository access","Immediately post the engineer's name and the download size in a company-wide alert","Delete the code repository to protect the data"],0,"UEBA surfaces deviations from a user's baseline. Legitimate access does not rule out misuse, so analysts investigate and involve HR and legal per policy.","Domain 4: Threat hunting",[null,"Authorized access can still be misused, and a huge spike after resignation is a classic exfiltration pattern.","Publicly naming someone before investigation is unfair and can create legal exposure.","Deleting the repository destroys business assets and evidence without addressing the behavior."],null],["sx77",0,4,"A scanner reports a CVSS 9.8 vulnerability on an isolated lab server and a CVSS 7.5 vulnerability that is listed in CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on an internet-facing VPN appliance. Which should be fixed first?",["The lab server, because its CVSS base score of 9.8 is higher than the other finding's","Both at the next quarterly patch window","Neither, until a penetration test confirms them","The VPN appliance, because it is exposed and the flaw is known to be exploited"],3,"Prioritization combines severity with exploitation evidence (KEV, EPSS), exposure and asset criticality. An actively exploited flaw on an internet-facing system is the more urgent risk.","Domain 4: Vulnerability management",["The CVSS base score alone ignores that the lab server is isolated and the other flaw is being exploited in the wild.","Waiting for a quarterly window leaves an actively exploited, internet-facing flaw open for too long.","Confirming a known exploited flaw with a pen test first only delays remediation.",null],null],["sx78",0,4,"A vulnerability scanner reports an outdated OpenSSL version on a Linux server, but the distribution has backported the security fix into its patched package. How should this finding be handled?",["Compile the newest OpenSSL from source, bypassing the distribution's package manager","Verify the package changelog or advisory, then document it as a false positive","Remove OpenSSL from the server","Accept the risk without checking"],1,"Distributions often backport fixes without changing the upstream version number, which fools version-based checks. Verify with the vendor advisory and record the exception.","Domain 4: Vulnerability management",["Building from source breaks vendor support and future updates for a problem that may already be fixed.",null,"Many system components depend on OpenSSL, so removing it would break the server.","Accepting without verification might leave a real vulnerability in place."],null],["sx79",0,4,"A web page builds a database query by concatenating user input. What is the most effective fix?",["Hide error messages from users","Block the word SELECT in input fields","Use parameterized queries (prepared statements)","Move the database to a different port"],2,"Parameterized queries keep code and data separate, so input is never interpreted as SQL. Input validation and least-privilege database accounts add defense in depth.","Domain 4: Vulnerabilities and attacks",["Hiding errors makes attacks harder to observe but does not remove the injection flaw.","Keyword blocklists are easy to evade and can block legitimate input.",null,"Changing the port does not change how the application builds queries."],null],["sx80",0,4,"A code review finds that a Java service accepts serialized objects from clients and reconstructs them without validation. Which vulnerability class does this create, and what is the fix?",["Cross-site scripting; encode output","Race condition; add a mutex","Directory traversal; canonicalize file paths and reject any path containing ../ sequences","Insecure deserialization; accept only data formats or allow-listed types"],3,"Deserializing untrusted objects can trigger dangerous code paths during object reconstruction. Prefer data-only formats like JSON with schema validation, or restrict deserialization to allow-listed classes.","Domain 4: Vulnerabilities and attacks",["XSS involves injecting script into web pages viewed by other users, not reconstructing objects on the server.","A race condition is a timing flaw between concurrent operations, not unsafe object reconstruction.","Directory traversal abuses file paths; this code issue is about deserializing untrusted data.",null],null],["sx81",0,4,"An application checks whether a user has enough balance and then deducts it in a separate step. Attackers submit many simultaneous requests and overdraw accounts. Which flaw is this?",["A buffer overflow","Cross-site request forgery","Broken TLS configuration","A race condition (time-of-check to time-of-use)"],3,"The gap between the check and the use lets concurrent requests all pass the check. Atomic transactions or locking fix it.","Domain 4: Vulnerabilities and attacks",["A buffer overflow writes past the end of a memory buffer; here the logic is correct per request but not atomic.","CSRF tricks a user's browser into sending a request; the problem here is concurrent requests racing each other.","TLS configuration affects transport encryption and would not let users overdraw balances.",null],null],["sx82",0,4,"A development team keeps shipping memory-corruption bugs in a C network parser. Which long-term engineering change most reduces this class of flaw?",["Rewrite it in a memory-safe language such as Rust and fuzz it continuously","Turn off compiler warnings to speed up builds","Increase the server's RAM","Run the parser as root so it never fails on permission errors when reading packets"],0,"Memory-safe languages eliminate whole classes of bugs such as buffer overflows and use-after-free. Continuous fuzzing finds remaining parser edge cases.","Domain 4: Vulnerabilities and attacks",[null,"Compiler warnings often point to memory bugs; turning them off hides problems.","More RAM does not prevent out-of-bounds writes or use-after-free errors.","Running as root makes any memory-corruption bug far more damaging."],null],["sx83",0,4,"An analyst wants to learn what a suspicious file does without letting it harm the network. Which approach is safest and still shows its behavior?",["Open it on the analyst's own workstation","Run it in an isolated sandbox and observe its behavior","Email it to colleagues for their opinion","Upload it to a public file-sharing site so other analysts can look"],1,"Dynamic analysis in an isolated sandbox reveals behavior safely. Static analysis (strings, headers, hashes) can come first without executing anything.","Domain 4: Malware analysis",["Running it on a production workstation risks infecting the machine and the network.",null,"Emailing malware spreads it to more people and systems.","Public uploads can leak sensitive data and tip off attackers."],null],["sx84",0,4,"A SOC wants to detect variants of a malware family that change their file hash on every build but share distinctive code and strings. Which tool fits?",["A list of SHA-256 hashes","A firewall rule that blocks port 80","YARA rules that match the shared patterns","A DMARC policy"],2,"YARA rules describe byte patterns, strings and conditions, catching variants that exact hashes miss.","Domain 4: Malware analysis",["Hash lists only match exact files, so each rebuilt variant slips past.","Blocking port 80 would break web traffic and does not identify malware files.",null,"DMARC controls email spoofing and has nothing to do with identifying malware files."],null],["sx85",0,4,"During a ransomware outbreak, the IR team has identified the infected hosts. According to the IR life cycle, what should happen next?",["Hold the lessons-learned meeting","Contain it by isolating affected hosts and disabling compromised accounts","Restore every affected system from last night's backup immediately, without further investigation","Reimage every computer in the company"],1,"After detection and analysis comes containment, which stops the spread. Eradication and recovery follow, and lessons learned come at the end.","Domain 4: Incident response",["Lessons learned belong to post-incident activity, after the incident is resolved.",null,"Restoring before containment and root cause analysis risks reinfection, and backups may themselves be compromised.","Reimaging every computer is disruptive, destroys evidence and is not the next step."],null],["sx86",0,4,"After eradicating malware, a team restores a server to production. What should the recovery phase include?",["Deleting all logs from the incident","Validating the system is clean and patched, then monitoring it closely","Turning off EDR on the restored server","Closing the incident ticket right away, since the malware has already been eradicated"],1,"Recovery restores service safely: verify integrity, confirm the root cause was fixed and watch for recurrence before declaring the incident closed.","Domain 4: Incident response",["Incident logs are evidence and are needed for lessons learned and possible legal action.",null,"EDR provides the monitoring needed to catch reinfection during recovery.","Closing without follow-up skips monitoring and the post-incident review."],null],["sx87",0,4,"Investigators must collect evidence from a running server suspected of hosting in-memory malware. Which should be captured first?",["Archived backup tapes","The server's hard disk image","System memory (RAM)","Paper printouts of the configuration"],2,"Following the order of volatility, memory is lost on shutdown, so it is captured before disk images and archival sources.","Domain 4: Forensics",["Backup tapes are among the least volatile sources and will still be there later.","Disk data survives a reboot and should be imaged after memory is collected.",null,"Printouts do not capture the in-memory malware, and configuration can be exported later."],null],["sx88",0,4,"A forensic image of a laptop will be used in court. What most helps prove it was not altered after collection?",["Storing the image on a shared network drive that the whole IR team can access","Renaming the file with the case number","Compressing the image to save space","Hashing it at acquisition and recording a chain of custody"],3,"A cryptographic hash taken at acquisition can be recomputed later to prove integrity, and chain-of-custody records show who handled the evidence and when.","Domain 4: Forensics",["A shared drive gives broad access and weakens assurance that nobody altered the image.","A file name does not prove anything about whether the contents changed.","Compression changes storage size but does not prove the evidence is unchanged.",null],null],["sx89",0,4,"A company acquired several subsidiaries and suspects there are internet-facing servers nobody knows about. Which activity addresses this first?",["Increasing password length requirements for all subsidiary accounts","Rewriting the acceptable use policy","Buying more SIEM storage","Discovering external domains, IP ranges and exposed services"],3,"You cannot protect assets you do not know about. Attack surface management discovers internet-facing assets so they can be inventoried, owned and secured.","Domain 4: Attack surface management",["Password policy changes do not reveal unknown internet-facing servers.","Policy updates do not locate assets that are missing from the inventory.","More SIEM storage helps only for assets already sending logs, which unknown servers are not.",null],null],["sx90",0,4,"A detection engineer writes a rule in a vendor-neutral YAML format so it can be converted into queries for several different SIEMs. Which format is this?",["Snort","STIX","Sigma","OpenIOC"],2,"Sigma is a generic signature format for log events that tools convert into SIEM-specific query languages.","Domain 4: Detection engineering",["Snort rules inspect network packets for an IDS/IPS, not log events across SIEMs.","STIX is a format for sharing threat intelligence, not writing log detection rules.",null,"OpenIOC is an XML format for describing indicators of compromise, not YAML log detection rules for SIEMs."],null]]);
